Here's a clean, general-purpose LaTeX template for an academic article. It uses the standard `article` class, so it will compile on Overleaf or any local TeX installation.

```latex
\documentclass[12pt, a4paper]{article}

% ---------- Packages ----------
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath, amssymb}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{setspace}
\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}
\usepackage[style=apa, backend=biber]{biblatex}
\addbibresource{references.bib}

\onehalfspacing

% ---------- Title Information ----------
\title{Your Article Title Here}
\author{
  Your Name \\
  Department of Your Field, Your University \\
  \texttt{your.email@university.edu}
}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A concise summary (150--250 words) of your research question,
methods, main findings, and conclusions.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} keyword one, keyword two, keyword three

\section{Introduction}
Introduce the topic, explain why it matters, and state your
research question or thesis.

\section{Literature Review}
Summarise relevant prior work \parencite{smith2020}.

\section{Methodology}
Describe your data, approach, and analytical methods.

\section{Results}
Present your findings. See Table~\ref{tab:results}.

\begin{table}[h]
  \centering
  \caption{Example results table}
  \label{tab:results}
  \begin{tabular}{lcc}
    \toprule
    Variable & Group A & Group B \\
    \midrule
    Measure 1 & 0.45 & 0.62 \\
    Measure 2 & 1.20 & 1.35 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Discussion}
Interpret your results and relate them to the literature.

\section{Conclusion}
Summarise contributions, limitations, and future directions.

\printbibliography

\end{document}
```

**A few notes:**

- **Bibliography:** Create a file called `references.bib` in the same folder with entries like:
  ```bibtex
  @article{smith2020,
    author  = {Smith, John},
    title   = {An Example Study},
    journal = {Journal of Examples},
    year    = {2020},
    volume  = {12},
    pages   = {34--56}
  }
  ```
- **Citation style:** Change `style=apa` to `ieee`, `mla`, `chicago-authordate`, or another style as your assignment requires.
- **Compiling:** Because it uses Biber, run LaTeX → Biber → LaTeX twice. Overleaf handles this automatically.
- **Figures:** Insert images with `\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{filename}` inside a `figure` environment.
